    "grade": "8-9",
    "points": 5,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "Of the $28$ T-shirts in a drawer, six are red, five are blue, and the rest are white. If Bob selects T-shirts at random whilst packing for a holiday, what is the least number he must remove from the drawer to be sure that he has three T-shirts of the same colour?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$4$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$13$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$9$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$19$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$7$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "E",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Pigeonhole",
        "body": "There are three colours, so in the worst case Bob has two T-shirts of each colour, $2\\times3=6$ T-shirts, with no three of the same colour. (There are at least two of each colour, since the white ones number $28-6-5=17$.) The $7$th T-shirt must give three of one colour.\n\n**Answer: (E) 7**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "counting",
      "logic"
    ],
    "difficulty": 2,
    "duplicateOf": null,
    "reviewNote": null,
    "wrongPoints": -1
  },
  {
