    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "In the diagram, $\\triangle ABC$ is equilateral. $\\triangle APQ$ is isosceles with $AQ=AP$ and $AQ\\parallel BC$. If $\\angle PAC=2x$, then the size of $\\angle Q$ in terms of $x$ is",
    "image": "qimages/samf/junior-2002-r2/q10.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$60^\\circ+x$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$180^\\circ+3x$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$120^\\circ-x$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$2x$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$60^\\circ-x$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "E",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Angle chase",
        "body": "Since $AQ\\parallel BC$, $\\angle QAC=\\angle ACB=60^\\circ$ (alternate angles). In the diagram $AP$ lies on the other side of $AC$ from $Q$, so $\\angle QAP=60^\\circ+2x$.\n\nTriangle $APQ$ is isosceles with $AQ=AP$, so the base angles at $P$ and $Q$ are equal: $\\angle Q=\\frac{180^\\circ-(60^\\circ+2x)}2=60^\\circ-x$.\n\n**Answer: (E) $60^\\circ-x$**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "geometry"
    ],
    "difficulty": 2,
    "duplicateOf": null,
    "reviewNote": null,
    "wrongPoints": -1
  },
